## Goal
Create a principal component analysis (PCA) of the RNA-seq samples using the gene counts, and plot the samples coloured by their recorded condition to show the main patterns of variation among samples.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` (native) | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
1. Run a Python script in the `gars-bio` environment through the configured batch executor. Read the `counts_gene` matrix and design table as inputs; do not modify either input.
2. Match count-matrix sample columns to `sample_id` rows in the design table and use each sample's `condition` for plot colour. Stop with an error if sample IDs are missing, duplicated, or do not match between the two inputs.
3. Treat count values as non-negative gene-level counts. Exclude genes with CPM < 1 in all but one sample, calculate CPM using each sample's total library size, transform retained values as `log2(CPM + 1)`, and center each gene across samples. Run PCA on the centered matrix, using all retained genes and scikit-learn `PCA` with two components. The PCA is descriptive and does not test condition differences.
4. Write a scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2 with one point per sample, coloured by condition and labelled with sample ID. Label axes with the percent variance explained. Also write a table of sample IDs, conditions, PC1 and PC2 scores, and a table of per-component explained variance. Save all outputs under this analysis directory's `results/`.

## Outputs
